1687: Nichola Sophia Hamilton, youngest daughter of Hugh Hamilton, 1st Viscount of Glenawly, married Sir Tristram Beresford. (background information)
  [
  Legend has it that as children, Nichola and John Power, 2nd Earl of Tyrone, agreed that whoever died first would return to report on the afterlife. (family legend)
  ]
  October 1693: Nichola wore a black ribbon and predicted the death of her friend John Power, Earl of Tyrone, based on a letter from his steward confirming his passing. (prediction)
  [
  Nichola also predicted the birth of Sir Tristram's son. (prediction)
  ]
  [
  When presented with documents changing her age to forty-seven, Nichola claimed she was dying. (prediction)
  ]
  [
  After the Earl of Tyrone's death, Nichola reported that his ghost visited her and revealed information about her future life. (ghostly encounter)
  ]
  [
  A black mark appeared on Nichola's wrist as proof that her ghostly encounter was real. (proof)
  ]
  [
  Nichola remarried General Richard Gorges of Kilbrew, County Meath, and had six more children. (remarriage)
  ]
  [
  Nichola died in 1713, possibly during childbirth with her youngest daughter Lucy, who later married William St Lawrence, 14th Baron Howth, and Nicholas Weldon. (death)
  ]
  [
  Nichola's half-sister Jane Beresford married Gorges Lowther, a prominent County Meath politician. Their descendants still hold the title Baron Crofton. (descendants)
  ]
  [
  General Gorges remarried Dorothy Stopford ("Countess Doll"), daughter of James Stopford and Mary Forth, and widow of the fourth Earl of Meath. (remarriage)
  ]
